WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.050
Welcome to Lecture 2 of the

2
00:00:02.115 --> 00:00:03.411
FrontierCS 2 course

3
00:00:03.411 --> 00:00:05.420
on high-performance computing.

4
00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:08.278
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

5
00:00:08.348 --> 00:00:10.090
removes its key idea from

6
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.693
the authors' own code

7
00:00:11.693 --> 00:00:14.410
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

8
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.759
This lecture has four tasks

9
00:00:16.759 --> 00:00:19.400
all from Julian Shun's group at MIT.

10
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.792
Their papers share one aim

11
00:00:21.792 --> 00:00:23.910
work-efficient parallelism.

12
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.909
Do no more total work than

13
00:00:25.972 --> 00:00:27.494
a good serial algorithm

14
00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:29.460
then spread it over the cores.

15
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.730
We start with RegionsSort.

16
00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.322
A radix sort puts every key in

17
00:00:34.389 --> 00:00:36.133
a bucket by its top digit

18
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:38.950
then sorts each bucket by the next digit.

19
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.210
The fast parallel versions copy

20
00:00:41.272 --> 00:00:43.058
the keys into a second array

21
00:00:43.058 --> 00:00:44.660
which doubles the memory.

22
00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.387
Regions Sort, from SPAA 2019

23
00:00:47.387 --> 00:00:48.699
works in place

24
00:00:48.699 --> 00:00:51.567
each key is swapped to its bucket

25
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:55.010
with as much work as a serial radix sort.

26
00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.207
Here is a toy of our own: forty keys

27
00:00:58.207 --> 00:01:00.612
four buckets by the top two bits

28
00:01:00.612 --> 00:01:02.310
and four blocks of ten.

29
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:03.099
First

30
00:01:03.099 --> 00:01:06.025
each block sorts its own keys by digit

31
00:01:06.025 --> 00:01:07.780
all blocks in parallel.

32
00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.145
Then counting gives each bucket

33
00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.290
its final interval, its country.

34
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.760
Country zero is positions zero to eight.

35
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.076
It should hold only zeros

36
00:01:18.076 --> 00:01:20.345
but it holds two ones, a two

37
00:01:20.345 --> 00:01:21.630
and four threes.

38
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.420
Each misplaced run of keys becomes

39
00:01:24.485 --> 00:01:26.370
an edge of the regions graph.

40
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.367
An edge from i to j with weight w says that

41
00:01:30.452 --> 00:01:33.760
w keys in country i belong to bucket j.

42
00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.654
Country zero holds seven foreign keys

43
00:01:36.721 --> 00:01:38.663
and seven keys of bucket zero

44
00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.270
sit in other countries.

45
00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.547
Weight in equals weight out

46
00:01:42.547 --> 00:01:43.855
for every country

47
00:01:43.855 --> 00:01:46.540
that is the lemma the paper builds on.

48
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.920
The paper plans swaps along two-paths.

49
00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.357
Take an edge from x to b

50
00:01:52.357 --> 00:01:54.110
and one from b to y.

51
00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.115
Swap equal parts of the two ranges

52
00:01:57.115 --> 00:01:58.860
and b's keys are home.

53
00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.248
The keys of y now sit in country x

54
00:02:02.248 --> 00:02:03.290
a new edge.

55
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:07.323
If y is x, both sides land home at once

56
00:02:07.323 --> 00:02:08.520
a two-cycle.

57
00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.154
Our toy plans one bucket at a time

58
00:02:11.154 --> 00:02:14.026
and a bucket's swaps touch disjoint ranges

59
00:02:14.026 --> 00:02:16.310
so they run in one parallel round.

60
00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.794
Ten swaps put all twenty-nine

61
00:02:18.868 --> 00:02:20.340
misplaced keys home.

62
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.818
The task keeps the sorter and

63
00:02:22.884 --> 00:02:24.520
removes the swap planner.

64
00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.752
Each submission sorts twelve

65
00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:28.230
arrays on four cores.

66
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.243
On every array, a pass needs a faster sort

67
00:02:31.307 --> 00:02:33.653
than the reference, a faster planner

68
00:02:33.716 --> 00:02:35.428
and extra memory of at most

69
00:02:35.491 --> 00:02:37.140
five percent of the input.

70
00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.084
The score comes from the speedup over

71
00:02:40.154 --> 00:02:42.607
the serial sort of the C++ library

72
00:02:42.607 --> 00:02:44.850
a tenfold speedup scores fifty.

73
00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.058
All sixteen runs had a faster planner than

74
00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.310
the reference on all twelve arrays.

75
00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.300
Pause and predict: how many passed?

76
00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:57.890
Only two.

77
00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.682
The planner is a small part of a sort

78
00:03:00.682 --> 00:03:02.225
about six milliseconds

79
00:03:02.225 --> 00:03:03.896
summed over four workers

80
00:03:03.896 --> 00:03:05.760
in a call of about nineteen.

81
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.999
Gemini 4 Argon, in Antigravity

82
00:03:08.999 --> 00:03:11.925
had the best score, fifty-one point five

83
00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:14.920
against fifty point two for the reference.

84
00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.192
It was faster on eleven of twelve arrays

85
00:03:18.192 --> 00:03:19.190
so it failed.

86
00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.219
Claude Opus 5.5 planned

87
00:03:21.292 --> 00:03:24.138
one bucket at a time, two-cycles first

88
00:03:24.211 --> 00:03:26.400
and deferred the graph updates

89
00:03:26.473 --> 00:03:27.860
to keep memory low.

90
00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:30.450
It was faster on seven arrays.

91
00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:32.630
Three things to take away.

92
00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.860
Misplaced ranges form a regions graph.

93
00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.082
A two-path gives one swap

94
00:03:38.082 --> 00:03:40.530
and a bucket's swaps run at once.

95
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.840
And a faster part is not a faster whole.

96
00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.350
As an exercise

97
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.596
plan the buckets in the order three, two

98
00:03:48.596 --> 00:03:51.070
one, zero, and count the swaps.

99
00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.900
Next, Parallel DBSCAN.
